NAME
    find - search a directory tree for names

SYNOPSIS
    find pattern [dir]

DESCRIPTION
    Walks the tree under the current directory (or dir) depth first and
    prints the absolute path of every file or directory whose name
    matches. A pattern with * or ? is a glob matched against the whole
    name, case folded; any other pattern matches the names that CONTAIN
    it, case-sensitively. A directory's contents follow it directly.
    "find: not a directory" when dir is not one. find is /BIN/FIND.

LIMITS
    8 levels below the start directory: a deeper directory is printed
    but not entered, and find says "find: deeper than 8 levels, not all
    searched". The pattern is cut at 63 characters.

EXAMPLES
    find .TXT            names containing ".TXT", from here down
    find *.txt /         every name ending in .TXT, from the root
    find C* /MAN         the pages whose names start with C
    find BIN /           where BIN is

NOTES
    Changed from the P8X version: an optional start directory, no limit
    of 24 subdirectories per level, and a note when the walk stopped
    short (the P8X was silent).
    Ported from P8X os/commands/find.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    tree, dir, grep, glob, walk
